1
00:00:02,798 --> 00:00:05,992
An assistant forgets everything between sessions.

2
00:00:05,992 --> 00:00:09,577
The usual fix is to embed the transcript and search it.

3
00:00:09,577 --> 00:00:13,814
That works, right up until the question depends on who said what.

4
00:00:13,816 --> 00:00:15,800
Three engineers pick Postgres.

5
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:17,785
One of them argues against it.

6
00:00:17,785 --> 00:00:20,761
In a later session they reverse the decision.

7
00:00:20,761 --> 00:00:26,318
A chunk index can find all of that text and still not tell you which decision stands

8
00:00:26,318 --> 00:00:27,376
or who objected.

9
00:00:27,379 --> 00:00:31,465
mnemex stores the argument instead of the transcript.

10
00:00:31,465 --> 00:00:32,544
People, claims

11
00:00:32,544 --> 00:00:37,555
decisions and reversals are all nodes and edges in a Neo4j graph.

12
00:00:37,558 --> 00:00:40,875
So we ask the question that flat retrieval cannot answer.

13
00:00:40,875 --> 00:00:44,542
What did we decide about the storage engine, and who disagreed?

14
00:00:44,543 --> 00:00:49,893
Everything memory knows comes back, the decisions that still stand first.

15
00:00:49,893 --> 00:00:52,018
Moving the datastore to Neo4j

16
00:00:52,018 --> 00:00:56,855
and it says plainly that this overrules the earlier Postgres call.

17
00:00:56,857 --> 00:00:59,406
Scroll down to the decision that lost.

18
00:00:59,406 --> 00:01:02,425
This is the part a vector store cannot reach.

19
00:01:02,425 --> 00:01:04,572
Marcus is named as the dissenter

20
00:01:04,572 --> 00:01:07,993
with the argument he actually made attached to him.

21
00:01:07,994 --> 00:01:10,909
The timeline tells the same story in order.

22
00:01:10,909 --> 00:01:15,722
Postgres first, over an objection, then the reversal that overruled it.

23
00:01:15,725 --> 00:01:17,650
Writing is a single tool call.

24
00:01:17,650 --> 00:01:22,013
A speaker, what they said, and the decision they took a position on.

25
00:01:25,958 --> 00:01:28,565
Embedding runs on a Nosana GPU.

26
00:01:28,565 --> 00:01:32,603
Entity resolution runs inside a Daytona sandbox.

27
00:01:32,603 --> 00:01:35,294
Then the graph updates in place.

28
00:01:35,295 --> 00:01:41,264
This is the memory itself, every node and edge it holds, across every session.

29
00:01:41,264 --> 00:01:42,795
Amber marks dissent.

30
00:01:42,795 --> 00:01:47,463
The dashed edges are decisions that overruled an earlier one.

31
00:01:47,465 --> 00:01:52,123
Click the decision that was overruled, and the graph isolates it.

32
00:01:52,123 --> 00:01:56,352
Who supported it, who disagreed, which session it came from

33
00:01:56,352 --> 00:01:57,857
and what replaced it.

34
00:01:57,859 --> 00:02:01,125
Four tools over the Model Context Protocol.

35
00:02:01,125 --> 00:02:04,314
Any assistant that speaks MCP gets durable

36
00:02:04,314 --> 00:02:08,491
relationship aware memory from one entry in its config.

37
00:02:08,493 --> 00:02:12,786
Ask what was decided, and get back what still stands, and who fought it.

38
00:02:12,786 --> 00:02:14,157
That is the whole idea.
